{
  "esm": "1.0.0",
  "metadata": {
    "name": "self_join_three_ranges_ambiguous",
    "description": "INVALID: the document does not determine which range symbol an `on` key column is read at (CONFORMANCE_SPEC.md §5.5.8 \"Two ranges over one index set\").\n\nThree ranges -- `a`, `b`, `c` -- all draw `{from: rows}`, and the key column `row_prior` is a declared 1-D variable over `rows`. Its axis therefore names THREE candidate range symbols and the pair `[\"row_prior\", \"row_id\"]` says nothing about which two the clause gates. The default side assignment is defined for exactly TWO candidates; with three, taking two of them would be a guess, and a guess here returns a plausible wrong number -- the wrong neighbour's value -- rather than a failure. The clause carries no `syms`, which is the spelling that would resolve it.\n\nThe defect is decidable STATICALLY from this ONE document -- the node's `ranges`, the document `index_sets`, the declared variable shapes and the clause itself are all here; no evaluation, no solver, no runtime data, no other file. `validate()` MUST reject it, in every binding, which is why it is pinned as a STRUCTURAL finding in tests/invalid/expected_errors.json rather than left to whichever pass a given implementation happens to run. The document is SCHEMA-VALID: the schema cannot express \"this key's axis is drawn by more than one range\".",
    "authors": [
      "EarthSciML Authors and Contributors"
    ],
    "tags": [
      "aggregate",
      "join",
      "self-join",
      "invalid",
      "build-time",
      "ambiguous-side"
    ]
  },
  "index_sets": {
    "rows": {
      "kind": "interval",
      "size": 5
    }
  },
  "models": {
    "ThreeRangesAmbiguous": {
      "variables": {
        "row_id": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "shape": [
            "rows"
          ],
          "description": "The relation's key column."
        },
        "row_prior": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "shape": [
            "rows"
          ],
          "description": "A shifted copy of `row_id` -- an ordinary 1-D data column over the SAME index set, which is what makes the clause a self-join."
        },
        "count": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "default": 0.0,
